    "slug": "how-to-use-a-case-converter",
    "title": "How to Use a Case Converter: camelCase, snake_case, PascalCase and More",
    "excerpt": "A practical guide to case conversion for developers, writers, and no-code users — when to use camelCase, snake_case, PascalCase, kebab-case and more.",
    "content": "**TL;DR:** If you write code, configure APIs, or manage database columns, you''ve probably needed to switch between `camelCase`, `snake_case`, `PascalCase`, and other styles. A good **case converter** handles that in one click — without changing your text''s meaning.\n\n---\n\n## What is a case converter?\n\nA **case converter** is a tool that changes the capitalization and separator style of text. It is not just about upper- and lowercase letters. Developers use it to normalize strings across systems that expect different naming conventions.\n\nCommon case styles:\n\n| Style | Example | Where it is used |\n|---|---|---|\n| `camelCase` | `userId`, `orderTotal` | JavaScript variables, JSON keys |\n| `PascalCase` | `UserId`, `OrderTotal` | React components, class names |\n| `snake_case` | `user_id`, `order_total` | Python, SQL columns, env vars |\n| `kebab-case` | `user-id`, `order-total` | CSS classes, URL slugs |\n| `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` | `USER_ID`, `API_KEY` | Constants, environment variables |\n| `Title Case` | `User Id`, `Order Total` | Headings, display labels |\n| `lowercase` | `user id` | Normalization, hashing |\n| `UPPERCASE` | `USER ID` | Emphasis, constants |\n\nA proper converter should also handle edge cases that break naive solutions:\n\n- consecutive separators like `user__id` → `user_id`\n- leading/trailing separators\n- numbers embedded inside words, e.g. `api2v1` → `api2v1`\n- mixed-case acronyms, e.g. `HTTPServer` → `http_server`\n\n---\n\n## Why you need one (even if you are not a developer)\n\nIf you work with:\n\n- **APIs**: different endpoints may require keys in different cases\n- **Spreadsheets**: export to CSV and import into systems with strict column-name rules\n- **CMS / no-code tools**: many platforms expect URL slugs or field names in `kebab-case`\n- **Localization**: convert user input into machine-friendly IDs before saving\n\nEven writers use case tools to transform titles, bullet lists, or hashtags into consistent formats.\n\n---\n\n## How to convert text between cases\n\nThe fastest way is to paste into a browser-based converter and click the desired format. Here is the practical workflow most people use:\n\n1. **Paste your raw text** into the input box.\n2. **Review mixed-case words** — especially acronyms like `HTTPS` or `ios`.\n3. **Click the target style** you need for your use case.\n4. **Copy the result** and reuse it.\n\nIf you are converting a list or table, convert one clean row first, verify it, then apply the same style to the rest.\n\n---\n\n## When to use each style\n\n| Use case | Recommended style |\n|---|---|\n| JavaScript / TypeScript variables | `camelCase` |\n| React components / classes | `PascalCase` |\n| Python / SQL | `snake_case` |\n| CSS classes / URLs | `kebab-case` |\n| Environment variables | `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` |\n| Page titles / UI labels | `Title Case` |\n| Human-readable identifiers | `lowercase` |\n\nGood consistency matters more than the exact style you choose. Pick one convention per project and stick to it.\n\n---\n\n## Differences between camelCase and PascalCase\n\n`camelCase` starts with a lowercase letter: `firstName`.\n\n`PascalCase` starts with an uppercase letter: `FirstName`.\n\nIn practice:\n\n- use `camelCase` for variables and functions\n- use `PascalCase` for classes and components\n- never mix both in the same namespace unless the language forces you to\n\n---\n\n## Does case conversion affect numbers?\n\nYes, but only if your tool strips them by mistake. A reliable converter preserves numbers inside words: `api2v1` stays `api2v1`, `user123` stays `user123`. If you need URL-safe output, pair case conversion with a slug generator.\n\n---\n\n## Can I convert a whole list at once?\n\nYes. Most useful case converters process batch input line by line. Paste a list, then convert all rows with one click rather than converting each row separately.\n\n---\n\n## Developer tips for naming conventions\n\n1. **Avoid Hungarian notation** — modern IDEs and TypeScript already give you type safety.\n2. **Keep names descriptive** — `u` is shorter, but `userId` is maintainable.\n3. **Use singular nouns for entities** — `user`, not `users`.\n4. **Use verbs for actions** — `getUser`, not `userGet`.\n5. **Align with your framework** — React prefers `PascalCase` for components, Vue accepts both but docs use `PascalCase`.\n\n---\n\n## FAQ\n\n**Does the converter preserve numbers inside words?**\n\nYes. A reliable converter keeps digits where they are. `api2v1` should remain `api2v1` after conversion.\n\n**What is dot.case?**\n\n`dot.case` uses periods instead of hyphens or underscores: `user.id`. Some legacy systems, email tags, or config formats use it.\n\n**Can I convert a whole list at once?**\n\nYes. Paste multiple lines and click the format you want. Most tools convert every line independently so your table structure stays intact.\n\n---\n\n## Conclusion\n\nA simple **case converter** saves time and avoids bugs when text moves between systems. Use it whenever you switch languages, frameworks, or APIs. If your current tool is stripping numbers, acronyms, or symbols, try a converter that treats edge cases as first-class citizens.\n\nIf you want to practice with one right now, open the [Case Converter on Nexatools](https://nexatools.cloud/tools/case-converter) and paste a sample list of API keys, CSS class names, or database columns.\n\n---\n\n## Further reading\n\n- [JSON formatting best practices every developer should know](https://nexatools.cloud/blog/json-formatting-best-practices)\n- [Base64 Encoder / Decoder](https://nexatools.cloud/tools/base64-encoder-decoder)\n- [JWT Decoder](https://nexatools.cloud/tools/jwt-decoder)\n\n---\n\n*This post explains how to use an online case converter and when to apply camelCase, snake_case, PascalCase, kebab-case, and related formats. For formatting, validation, and encoding tools, visit [Nexatools](https://nexatools.cloud).*",
