    "slug": "json-formatting-best-practices",
    "title": "JSON formatting best practices every developer should know",
    "excerpt": "Consistent JSON formatting saves debugging hours and prevents subtle bugs in APIs, configs, and logs. Here are the rules we use — and how to enforce them with the Nexatools JSON Formatter.",
    "content": "# JSON formatting best practices\n\nJSON is deceptively simple. A single trailing comma or an unquoted key can bring down a deploy at 2 AM. Here are the seven rules we follow on every project — and how the [Nexatools JSON Formatter](/tools/json-formatter) helps you enforce them without leaving the browser.\n\n## 1. Pick one indentation and stick with it\n\nTwo spaces is the de-facto standard for JSON in web ecosystems. Four is fine for large config files. Tabs are legal but many tools mangle them. Whatever you pick — commit it to your linter config and never mix.\n\n## 2. Sort keys deterministically\n\nDiffs get 10× easier to review when keys are sorted alphabetically. Most JSON linters have a `--sort-keys` flag. Do it.\n\n## 3. No trailing commas — but comment them in your `.jsonc`\n\nStrict JSON forbids trailing commas. VS Code style `.jsonc` allows them. Pick per file type and don't cross the streams.\n\n## 4. Always quote keys with double quotes\n\nSingle quotes are legal JavaScript, not legal JSON. Common pitfall when pasting from a REPL.\n\n## 5. Prefer ISO 8601 for dates\n\n`\"2026-07-19T14:32:00Z\"` beats `1721399520` for readability and beats `\"07/19/2026\"` for portability.\n\n## 6. Minify only at the network boundary\n\nStore formatted JSON in your repo (diffable), minify at build time or on the wire.\n\n## 7. Validate before you commit\n\nA broken JSON config that reaches production is the most avoidable outage on Earth. Every JSON file should pass `JSON.parse` before it enters `main`.\n\n## Try it now\n\nPaste any JSON into the [Nexatools JSON Formatter](/tools/json-formatter) — it prettifies, minifies, sorts keys, and flags syntax errors with a line number. Everything runs in your browser, so it works with the most sensitive payloads too.",
